# CODEOWNERS — paired with `require_code_owner_reviews` on the `main`
# branch-protection rule. GitHub resolves this file by walking the
# repository from the changed file up to the root and taking the **last**
# matching pattern, so order matters: put more-specific patterns after
# less-specific ones. A reviewer approval from any listed owner satisfies
# the rule for a PR.
#
# Two-owner project: every path is owned by @greysquirr3l and
# @sosh-ncampbell. Both accounts have write access to the repository, so
# either one can satisfy the code-owner review requirement on its own.
#
# The reason there are two owners rather than one is the interaction
# between two rules in the `main` ruleset (`rulesets/25918976`):
#
#   - `require_code_owner_review` — the approval must come from someone
#     named in this file.
#   - `require_last_push_approval` — the approval must come from
#     someone *other* than the account that made the last push.
#
# With a single owner, those two rules are mutually unsatisfiable for
# any PR that owner pushes: the only code owner is also the last
# pusher, so no approval can clear both. With two owners the pusher and
# the approver can be different people, and either one is a code owner.
# That is the arrangement the ruleset is asking for.
#
# Both owners must therefore appear on *every* rule below, not just the
# default. GitHub takes the last matching pattern and uses only that
# pattern's owners, so a rule listing just @greysquirr3l would drop
# @sosh-ncampbell from the ownership of those paths and re-create the
# deadlock for changes to them. The security-critical sections below
# repeat both owners explicitly so future reviewers see them highlighted
# in the PR Owners panel rather than collapsing into the default
# fallback line, and so that splitting a path out into its own rule
# later is a one-line edit instead of a hunt.

# Default — every path is owned by both project owners.
/*    @greysquirr3l @sosh-ncampbell

# Security-critical surface — release pipeline, SBOM, branch protection
# automation, signing, and security policy. Reviewed by both owners so
# that neither can land a change to these paths alone.
/.github/workflows/release.yml           @greysquirr3l @sosh-ncampbell
/.github/workflows/scorecard.yml         @greysquirr3l @sosh-ncampbell
/.github/dependabot.yml                  @greysquirr3l @sosh-ncampbell
/.github/CODEOWNERS                      @greysquirr3l @sosh-ncampbell
/docs/security-audit.md                  @greysquirr3l @sosh-ncampbell
/SECURITY.md                             @greysquirr3l @sosh-ncampbell

# Cryptographic / supply-chain configuration — Rust toolchain pin, deny
# license/advisory policy, and Cargo's own lockfile.
/rust-toolchain.toml                     @greysquirr3l @sosh-ncampbell
/deny.toml                               @greysquirr3l @sosh-ncampbell
/Cargo.lock                              @greysquirr3l @sosh-ncampbell
